WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.809
Depending on the pathway analysis approach
that you use to analyze your data, that can

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:15.260
have a dramatic impact on the type of insights
that you're able to derive from your results.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:19.980
Let's look at how these different analysis
approaches differ from one another.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.289
The first and most common approach is what's
called over-representation analysis.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:29.109
It's most commonly referred to as enrichment
analysis.

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:33.710
This is a very simple but powerful technique
that leverages simple statistics to identify

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.420
pathways that may be significant given your
data.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.920
The other benefit to this particular approach
is that it doesn't require very much input

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.340
data.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.900
You can submit a short list of genes that
you're interested in and you'll get a list

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.390
of pathways that are enriched for that set
of genes.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:55.840
The downside to this method is that it makes
an assumption on the background of those genes.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.710
Because you only submitting a short list of
genes, it has to make an assumption of what

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.780
is the denominator.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.030
For example, if you have a hundred significant
genes the system might not know what was the

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.300
total number of genes that has been analyzed.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.810
In other word, is it hundred significant genes
out of ten thousand, twenty thousand or is

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.720
it fifty thousand.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.430
So it makes some assumptions on that.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:22.760
The other downside to this method is that
you end up throwing away about 90% of your

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.250
data.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.120
For example, if you've done an expression
experiment where you have observations for

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.120
20,000 genes, usually there's only about a
thousand or 2,000 that you're interested in,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.390
out of that entire set.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:40.920
That is a major disadvantage because you spend
a lot of time and money in getting those measurements.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.260
The biggest limitation of this method is that
it assumes that all of the genes in the pathway

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.230
are independent.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.890
It completely ignores the interactions among
genes within the pathway.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:57.610
This can lead to a lot of false positives
A slight improvement on enrichment analysis

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.840
approach is what's called functional class
scoring.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.049
You may have encountered gene set enrichment
analysis or GSEA which was pioneered by the

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:08.080
Broad Institute.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.450
In this particular model, it is more accurate
than the simple enrichment model because in

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.780
this method we're going to use the entire
list of genes that were measured.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:21.650
In the case of gene set enrichment analysis
we will also supply the p-values and supply

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.760
them a ranked order.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.739
Depending on the type of functional class
scoring method, you may provide some weighted

00:02:27.739 --> 00:02:29.090
scoring based on those p-value.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:35.310
But, because this is still based on an enrichment
model, it is still going to ignore the interactions

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.950
among the genes on the pathway.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:44.019
It is still a count-based model and as a result
this method can lead to a lot of false positives.

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:48.930
In the mid 2000, a completely different type
of approach was proposed.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:54.099
Its goal was to mimic how we think about pathways
from a biological perspective.

00:02:54.099 --> 00:02:58.420
In this case we're trying to identify how
perturbed a pathway is given a particular

00:02:58.420 --> 00:02:59.779
input.

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:03.700
We can do that by leveraging the topology
of the pathway.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:08.260
In this particular method, we're going to
consider each gene’s role, their position,

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.159
the magnitude of change between our two phenotypes,
and the interactions among other genes in

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.459
that particular system.

00:03:15.459 --> 00:03:19.239
One of the advantage about this particular
model is that it's able to predict what's

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:23.099
going to occur in that system given the observations
from the data.

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:26.719
However, there are also some downsides to
this method.

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:31.360
First, it requires more data in this particular
model.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.439
We need to have measurements for all of the
genes between the two phenotypes, their measured

00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:38.219
fold change, and their p-value.

00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:43.370
When this method was proposed, that was a
very difficult and expensive task.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:49.299
But with advents and improvements in transcriptomics
and proteomics technology, and bioinformatics,

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:52.169
it is a much easier to accomplish.

00:03:52.169 --> 00:03:56.230
This method does take slightly longer than
the enrichment models.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:01.529
The enrichment models can be calculated in
the matter of seconds or less, but the topology-based

00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:06.109
model is very computationally intensive and
takes anywhere from five to ten minutes to

00:04:06.109 --> 00:04:06.799
calculate.

